# Documentation Prose Style

This rule governs the words in every `.rst` and `.md` file under `docs/`, plus
`README.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md`. It sits alongside `doc_user_guide.mdc` (which
governs what a user-guide chapter contains) and `doc_dev_guide.mdc` (which governs
the developer guide). Where this rule and those overlap, this one governs the
sentence and that one governs the outline.

`scripts/check-doc-prose.sh` enforces the mechanically detectable half. Run it
before claiming a documentation change is done; `scripts/run-all-checks.sh` runs
it too. The rest of this rule is a review checklist -- a human or a model has to
read the prose to apply it.

## 1. Audience separation

The user guide is for users. A user runs command-line programs, reads the files
those programs write, and reasons about images, missions, cameras, rings, and
bodies. A user has never read our source and cannot open a file inside the
installed package.

**Never in the user guide:**

- Class names used as objects (`NavResult`, `ObsSnapshotInst`).
- Attribute or field access (`nav_result.offset_px`, `obs.star_max_usable_vmag`,
  `psf_model.eval_rect`, `stars.max_smear`).
- Function names (`navigate_image_files`, `build_metadata_dict`,
  `compute_pointing`, `select_pointing`, `apply_pointing_to_obs`).
- Packaged configuration filenames (`config_050_rings.yaml` and its siblings).
  The configuration chapter states where the packaged defaults live, **once**, as
  a parenthetical aside noting that this is where developers find them. No other
  chapter repeats it.

**Legitimately user-facing, and therefore allowed:**

- Names a user types on the command line. Model and technique names
  (`NavModelStars`, `BodyLimbNav`) are the patterns passed to `--nav-models` and
  `--nav-techniques`. Say a technique is **named after the class that implements it**;
  never that it "registers under" a name, and never use the word **registry** --
  a user does not know what one is and does not need to.
- Keys a user reads in a metadata document. Name them as JSON keys, not as
  Python attributes.
- Module and class names in a chapter that documents an importable package. Say
  up front that the chapter documents an importable package, cover the
  command-line programs first, and put the module material in its own file.

Where the machinery genuinely matters to a user's understanding, name the
technique in one plain sentence and cross-reference the developer guide with
`:doc:` instead of explaining it in place.

## 2. Forbidden constructions

| Do not write | Write instead | Why |
|---|---|---|
| "marginal frames are refused, not guessed" | "marginal frames are refused" | See the X-not-Y test below. |
| "one kind of refusal is recorded nowhere" | "this kind of refusal is not recorded" | You cannot record something nowhere. |
| "an image with no usable pointing" | "an image that has no usable pointing" | You cannot be *with* an absent thing. |
| "an image the results index has no row for" | "an image that has no row in the results index" | No inverted relative clause with a stranded preposition. |
| "an image whose metadata document the ingest could not read" | "an image whose metadata document could not be read" | Same. |
| "one image that cannot be processed is not in the run" | "an image that cannot be processed is not in the run" | "one" reads as a count. |
| "the frame passes the system-level gate" | "the frame is accepted only when its uncertainty is below N pixels" | Name what is tested. |
| "the document shape depends on ..." | "the metadata document's contents depend on ..." | A shape is a physical form. |
| "exactly as every offset product has always been built" | (delete) | No implementation history. |
| "every program accepts the same options, so what you learn for one works for the next" | "every program accepts the same options" | Delete clauses that restate the obvious. |

### The X-not-Y test

Keep "X, not Y" only when **Y is something a reasonable reader might genuinely
expect to happen**, so that ruling it out does real work. If no reasonable person
would ever assume Y, the contrast is noise: state X alone.

- Keep: "the offset is measured against the furnished kernels, not against a
  fixed reference" -- a reader might well have assumed a fixed reference.
- Delete the contrast: "marginal frames are refused, not guessed" -- nobody
  expects a guess.

### The gate rule

Do not use **gate** to mean "meets a criterion". It is not how the word works in
ordinary English. Replace it with wording that names exactly what is being
tested. Do not sacrifice clarity or accuracy to avoid the word -- rewrite the
sentence instead.

### The shape rule

**shape** means dimensions and nothing else. `image shape` (rows by columns) is
correct and stays. A metadata document has **contents** or a **structure**.

## 3. Terminology

- **Never bare "document".** Write **metadata document**, **PDS4 label**, or
  whatever it actually is.
- **Never bare "index".** There are two, and they are unrelated: the **results
  index** (our one row per navigated image) and the **PDS3 index** (the index
  table shipped with a PDS3 volume). Every mention says which. When only one is
  relevant, name that one and do not mention the other.
- **PDS3 is not an image format.** It is an archive organization. Cassini ISS,
  Voyager ISS, and Galileo SSI images are **VICAR**; New Horizons LORRI images
  are **FITS**.
- The corrected **pointing** is what a downstream consumer uses. The **offset**
  is a correction relative to the SPICE kernels furnished when the image was
  navigated; wherever an offset is described, name those kernels, because without
  them the offset means nothing.
- **corotation** and **corotating** take no hyphen.
- American spelling throughout. `codespell` fails the build otherwise; see
  `CLAUDE.md` for the allow-list policy.
- **Define a term before using it.** A chapter that uses *feature*, *polarity*,
  *confidence*, *curator*, *identity key*, or *rank deficiency* must have
  introduced it, or must link to the chapter that does.

## 4. Sentence mechanics

- Short declarative sentences. No stacked subordinate clauses, no semicolon
  chains. A convoluted paragraph becomes two or three plain sentences.
- Ground an explanation in real images and real geometry rather than in
  abstractions.
- **Oxford comma, always.** Every list of three or more items separated by "and"
  or "or" takes a comma before the conjunction.
- Do not describe a limitation as fundamental when it is a convention. Splitting a
  large mission into chunks is a convenience that lets each chunk's success be
  assessed before the next one starts; nothing limits how many images one run can
  process.
- Do not include a hypothetical a user would never meet -- a hand-built record in
  the results tree, a batch that did not contain exactly one image.
- Issue numbers appear only in `..` comments, never in running prose.

